export const essays = {
  "guides/dachshund-health-issues.html": `<p>A yelp when I scoop a long dog off the rug is usually the spine, and I treat it that way until proven otherwise. Standards I weigh sit about 16 to 32 pounds. Miniatures stay at 11 pounds and under. Both builds carry a long spine on short legs, and intervertebral disc disease is the injury that defines them. I have carried one to the car who ate breakfast standing and could not push up by lunch. The back may roach. The hind toes may drag. Sometimes the only clue is a flinch when a hand slides under the belly.</p>
<p>Inside the house the same injury is the launch off a sofa or a bed. Front feet hit, the hind end follows from a height, and the disc takes a shock it was never built to take. Ramps stay on any furniture I leave available. I lift with a palm under the ribs and a palm under the pelvis so the column stays level. A harness puts the leash on the chest. Neck leads hang on a hook by the door.</p>
<p>At year 3 I ask for a neurologic exam even when the trot looks even. At year 5 I ask again if stairs have turned into a negotiation. Acute weakness belongs at the hospital the same day, with a note on whether urine is still passing. Imaging is the doctor's decision once the dog is in front of them. The timeline helps: first yelp, any knuckling, any cry when the loin is touched.</p>
<p>The years I quote families are 12 to 16. A blown disc is what steals the easy ones inside that span. Pounds past the standard band, or past 11 on a miniature, load the same discs. A kitchen scale lives in my cupboard and the number goes on the calendar. During a recovery the stairs close and the crate exit is one low step. I have seen a second episode in the same season when a dog who looked sound was allowed back onto the bed.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck's disc disease notes for long-backed hounds</a> is the reference I text when someone wants the medical name for the dog who suddenly could not walk. The harness goes on before the leash, including the short trip to the yard.</p>`,

  "guides/dachshund-temperament.html": `<p>A steak on the floor lost to a mole tunnel in my yard. That is the hound I kept: nose first, opinion second, volume always available. Earthdog work is the old job, going to ground after quarry that lived in a hole. In a rental the same drive becomes barking at a fresh scent and digging along the fence where the smell went under. I built a dig box from a plastic pool and packed dirt so the claws had a legal target. The lawn survived. The instinct did not retire.</p>
<p>Scent games land on session 3 and again on session 5 of my week. I hide a smelly cloth under a towel, then under a box, then along the baseboard. The dog who finishes those puzzles barks less at the window. The voice itself is a chopped bay, loud for the body, and neighbors hear the alert before I reach the hall. Punishment did nothing useful. A job for the nose did.</p>
<p>Independence sits in the breed history. This is a dog who went underground alone and made decisions without a handler in the hole. Recall off a hot trail is a skill I practice on a long line, with a jackpot of food at my knee, and I still expect the nose to argue. Brave is a fair word. Reckless around a strange dog's face is the shadow of that bravery. I introduce visitors with the Dachshund on a leash until the barking settles into a sniff.</p>
<p>Puppies scream-bark at leaves. Adults pick their alarms with more purpose and the same lungs. I still give the adult a scent problem every evening. An idle earthdog hires himself, and the self-assigned work is a hole by the gate or a concert at dusk. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/dachshund/">AKC Dachshund hound-and-earthdog profile</a> matches the animal on my couch: a hunter who expects the ground to hold a secret, and who will tell the whole block when he thinks he found it.</p>`,

  "guides/dachshund-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Three jackets live under one breed name, and my hands change with each. Smooth coats fool people. A rubber curry on a standard whose scale reads 16 to 32 still picks up grit along the sternum, and the belly skin needs a look because it rides close to wet grass. Nails on a short-legged dog click early. I clip them while the dog is already standing for the curry. A miniature under 11 pounds has less barrel and the same nail math.</p>
<p>Longhaired furnishings are a different sit. I comb the ear fringe from the leather outward, then the chest feather, then the tail flag. Mats hide behind the ears and in the armpit. A slicker that yanks will make the next session a fight, so I hold the hair under the snarl and work that snarl loose. Baths are occasional. Water has to reach the skin under that feathering, and the towel has to finish the ears or they stay damp.</p>
<p>Wire coats want fingers more than clippers. I hand-strip the blown jacket in small pinches, lifting the dead harsh hair so the new coat can stand up. Clippers left on the body turn that jacket soft and dull, and then dirt sticks. My log keeps a coat check at month 12 and another at month 16. Pass 3 and pass 5 on the wire chest are the ones I slow down for, because the skin is thin there and the strip should never scrape.</p>
<p>Teeth and the fold under a long ear get checked in the same session. Smooth, long, or wire, the grooming sit is when I find a lump or a rash before it becomes a phone call. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/dachshund">VCA coat notes on the Dachshund</a> line up with what my hands already know: the variety of hair changes the tool, and the skin underneath still needs a person who is actually looking.</p>`,

  "guides/dachshund-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Flat ground and a nose job are the week I can defend. The old work was going to ground and following scent, so I build a small version of that and I leave the jumping out. Outing 3 is a line of boxes in the hallway, each with a bit of hot dog under a rag, harness on, no stairs. Outing 5 is a dig box on the porch and a slow sniff walk on grass. Both end while the dog still wants one more turn.</p>
<p>The other days stay shorter. One morning is a harness walk around the block with permission to investigate every tree. One evening is a chew and a nap, because the long back has spent itself. I skip fetch that launches off the couch. I skip agility contacts. A ramp to the car replaces the hop into the hatch. If the back has ever seized, the week shrinks to scent work on the floor and the walk gets flatter.</p>
<p>Heat is a small problem beside the spine, but a long dog in full sun still needs water and shade. I watch the hind footfalls. A scrape of the nails that was absent yesterday ends the outing. Young dogs get the same shapes with less time while the growth plates and the discs are both unfinished. Adults who are sound can have a longer Saturday sniff in a field, still on a line, still without a jump.</p>
<p>A tired mind is the point. A frantic lap of the yard leaves the nose unemployed and the back annoyed. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/dachshund/">AKC field habits of the Dachshund</a> describe a hunter, and a hunter on my calendar gets puzzles, dirt, and rest, in that order, every week I am home to run it.</p>`,

  "guides/dachshund-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Paperwork comes out before any deposit leaves my hand. I want the parents' spinal history in writing, a note on whether either adult has been off its feet, and any orthopedic file the kennel already holds. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC paperwork checklist for Dachshund parents</a> is the stack I know how to read. A seller who waves that off is a seller I leave. Two sizes exist. I ask which one this litter is aimed at, standard or miniature, before I fall for a puppy face.</p>
<p>Week 3 in my house is sleep, a low crate, and a harness clipped for the trip to the grass. Week 5 the crate nap after meals is already boring, which is the goal, and I start feeding from my hand so fingers are good news. The body lengthens early. The legs stay short. That shape is the adult, arriving in miniature. Sofas are off limits from the first night. I pick the puppy up with two hands. A jump off the cushion is the household version of a disc injury, practiced young.</p>
<p>Teething keeps the mouth busy. I trade a toy for the shoe and I teach a settle on a mat that sits on the floor, never on the furniture. Digging shows up as soon as the claws are long enough to matter. A pie tin of dirt on a towel saves the houseplant. Barking shows up just as fast. A short scent game beats a lecture. By the middle of the first year I know whether the coat is smooth, long, or wire, and the grooming tool matches that, while the scale tells me whether we are heading for the standard band or staying tiny.</p>
<p>Nails, ears, and a stand for a fake exam happen every week while the puppy is still easy to hold. The first year is manners, a harness habit, and papers already in the folder, so the long back has a head start on careful handling.</p>`,

  "guides/dachshund-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Hair on a black shirt comes from three different jackets, and I can usually tell which Dachshund I brushed. Smooth coats drop short single hairs from the torso, the thighs, and the rim of the ear. They shed a little every day. You notice it on fleece and in the washer lint, a pepper of hair that was growing out of the outer jacket yesterday.</p>
<p>Longhaired dogs shed from the undercoat hiding inside the furnishings. The ear fringe, the chest feather, and the tail flag are where I pull loose down with a comb. That fluff drifts onto the couch arm and clogs a bathroom drain after a bath. It is softer than the smooth-coat needle and it mats if I leave it in the feathering. Brush 3 and brush 5 of my week are the longhair days, and the hair in the comb is the shed I kept off the floor.</p>
<p>Wire coats hold the dead jacket until it blows or until I strip it. The hair that comes out in my fingers is harsh and banded, grown from the outer coat. A standard whose card reads 16 to 32 has more surface than a miniature capped at 11 pounds, so the same follicle pattern fills a larger lint trap. I note the coat at month 12 and again at month 16, because a young wire jacket changes texture as the puppy coat leaves and the adult harsh coat comes in. That transition is when people think the dog suddenly started shedding. The old coat was ready to leave.</p>
<p>Vacuum the edges of the bed. Long dogs sleep stretched, and the contact points are where the hair lands. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/dachshund">VCA Dachshund grooming background</a> describes the three coats, and the three coats are why one household's lint roller looks nothing like another's.</p>`,

  "guides/dachshund-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>I quote 12 to 16 years, and the next sentence is about the back. Intervertebral disc disease is what cuts the easy years out of that span. I have signed charts on dogs who were comic and fast at eight and who needed a cart, or a grave, after one bad jump off a bed. The ones who reached the far side of 16 had ramps, a harness, and a person who treated a yelp as an emergency.</p>
<p>Checkup 3 and checkup 5 are when I want a neurologic look even if the family thinks the gait is normal. Stairs, repeated hopping into a car, and pounds stacked on the ribs are the household habits that shorten the line. A miniature under the small ceiling and a standard in the larger band share the same disc risk. The smaller dog is easier to lift. The physics of a long spine do not grant a free pass to either size.</p>
<p>Coat type does not set the clock. Smooth, long, and wire dogs in my files age by their backs and their teeth more than by their hair. Dental disease left alone makes the later years miserable, so the grooming sit stays on the calendar into old age. A dog who cannot walk a flat block has a different life from a dog who can, even when both are still eating. That difference is the one I am trying to protect.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/dachshund/">AKC years-lived notes on Dachshunds</a> sit beside the span I already quote. Families who want the far end of 12 to 16 keep the dog off the furniture and the weight on a scale they actually read.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-dachshunds-get.html": `<p>Two sizes share the name. A standard adult lands at a registry weight of 16 to 32 pounds. A miniature stays at a ceiling of 11 pounds. I have stood them side by side in the clinic hallway: same long back, same short legs, different mass. Coat does not move the category. Smooth, longhaired, and wirehaired dogs are weighed the same way, on a scale, standing square enough that the chest is over the platform.</p>
<p>The puppy does not look finished, even when the shape is already obvious. Length shows up before the chest fills. At month 3 the feet and the keel are the whole impression. At month 5 the appetite surges and people declare the dog done because the legs stopped looking longer. They were always going to be short. I write the weights down. Memory turns every pup into the biggest in the litter, or the tiniest, depending on the story the seller told.</p>
<p>Between month 8 and month 12 a small breed's frame usually looks adult to a stranger. I still keep a note near month 16, because some standards put on the last of the rib spring after the height of the shoulder has stalled. Miniatures often settle earlier and simply stay under that 11 pound cap. Jumping stays off the couch through the whole stretch. A ramp to the sofa is easier to teach at month 8 than to invent after a disc injury at month 12.</p>
<p>Ribs on a long body are easy to misread. I want a waist from above and a tuck behind the ribs, on the standard inside 16 to 32 and on the miniature under 11. A thick layer over the loin loads the spine I am trying to keep quiet for 12 to 16 years. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/dachshund/">AKC Dachshund size and variety page</a> is the card in my puppy folder so a photo stops being the measuring tool.</p>
<p>Buyers who only met the dam on a lap misread mass. I set the dog on the scale after a walk, before a meal, and I write both context and pounds. Toes stretched for a treat can fake a taller dog. Withers on a Dachshund were never the point. The point is the pounds on that long chassis, and whether the puppy in the pen is aimed at the standard band or the miniature cap.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-health-issues.html": `<p>Brown crumbs in a long drop ear, plus a head shake that spatters the cabinet, are how ear infections introduce themselves in the Beagles I have kept. The leather hangs heavy, the canal stays warm, and yeast finds a home. I wipe the visible ear and I want the clinic to look inside with a scope before anyone dumps a random bottle in. Smell is the home clue. Pain when the ear base is touched is the clue that this one cannot wait.</p>
<p>Seizures are the face of epilepsy here. I ask the family for a written log: how long the event ran, whether the legs paddled, how fast the dog came back, and what happened in the hour before. A thyroid panel belongs on the list too, because hypothyroidism shows up as a coarse coat, a dog who seeks the heat vent, and the same scoop of food starting to pad the ribs. Cherry eye is the pink gland popping at the inner corner. I photograph it and book an eye exam before the surface dries out.</p>
<p>Intervertebral disc disease shows up as a hunched little hound who refuses the car and cries when lifted. That is a same-day problem, separate from the ordinary ear visit. Musladin-Lueke syndrome has DNA testing in some lines. I ask the breeder what the parents' results were, and I ask again if a young dog moves with a stiff, tight-skinned gait that does not match a sore paw. Visit 3 and visit 5 of the first year are when I want those questions already answered on paper.</p>
<p>The appetite will outrun the bowl. Obesity is the slow injury I can see on the scale: ribs buried, waist gone, a hound who still begs like he missed a meal. I measure every portion. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/beagle">VCA Beagle disease rundown</a> is the page I leave on the fridge beside the measuring cup, because the ear, the eye, the thyroid, the back, and the seizures are a list, and the list is easier to face when it has names.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-temperament.html": `<p>The nose hires the rest of the dog. I have watched a Beagle in the 20 to 30 pound band go deaf to his name the moment a rabbit crossed the ditch, bay rising, tail rigid, whole body locked onto a scent trail the rest of us could not see. That is a scenthound. Pack life is the other half. Alone in a yard he sings. With another dog he sings louder. The voice is a bay and a howl, and it carries.</p>
<p>Food is the lever that works. A pocket of kibble buys a recall I could not purchase with volume. I pay the sit, I pay the check-in, and the trail still wins sometimes. Fences are a suggestion when the smell is hot. I have filled a dig-out under chain link and watched the same dog test the gate latch one hour later. A long line is how we hunt together until the recall is real. A buried fence wire did less for me than a locked gate and a person at the latch.</p>
<p>Session work on day-count matters. Trail games sit on mark 3 and again on mark 5 of the week, short enough that he finishes wanting another turn, real enough that the nose is tired. The years I have shared with these hounds run from year 10 toward year 15, and the nose does not retire when the muzzle grays. A senior still needs a snuffle mat or he will invent a trail along the neighbor's compost.</p>
<p>Puppies scream-bay at nothing. Adults pick a scent and commit. Both want company. A Beagle exiled to a pen becomes a singer with a grievance. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/beagle/">AKC Beagle scenthound portrait</a> matches the hound who sleeps on my feet and vanishes, mentally, the second the window brings in deer. Two height varieties exist, 13 inches and under, and over 13 up to 15. The shorter variety hunts with the same commitment. The nose does the hiring in both.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-grooming-guide.html": `<p>A short coat invites neglect, and the neglect shows up in the ears. I run a hound glove over the ribs and the thighs on cycle 3 and again on cycle 5. Loose white and tri hairs lift into the fabric. The glove takes five quiet minutes. The ear takes the real attention. I lift the long leather, wipe the visible flap with a damp cloth, and smell the canal. Sweet or yeasty means the clinic sees it before I pretend a home bottle is a plan.</p>
<p>Baths stay uncommon. Mud on a Beagle rinses once the water reaches the skin. Mild shampoo, a rinse until it sheets, then a towel that finishes the ears and the groin. A damp drop ear after a bath is how I have started infections I then had to treat. Nails click on the kitchen floor sooner than people think, because the body is low and the nails meet the ground on every step. I clip during the glove session so nail day is never a separate argument.</p>
<p>Teeth get a finger brush while he is already standing for food. The lips lift easily on this head. Feet get handled every time, including the webs where a burr hides after a field edge. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/beagle">VCA Beagle short-coat care</a> matches the routine on my porch: a simple jacket, and a pair of heavy ears that will punish anyone who only brushes the back.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A trail is the original job, so the week is nose work plus a fence I trust. Monday I lay a drag with a hot dog through the yard and let him work it on a long line. The bay is allowed. The dash through an open gate is not part of the plan, so the gate is latched before the line comes off the hook. Wednesday is a sniff walk where he may investigate every clump of grass and I do not rush the cast.</p>
<p>The longer hunt sits on slot 3. A recovery trot, loose and boring, sits on slot 5. Between them he gets a pack walk with a steady dog if I have one, because a Beagle settles better beside another body than in a silent house. Sunday is rest: a chew, a snuffle mat, no new trail. Food comes in a puzzle on the rest day so the appetite has a job when the legs do not.</p>
<p>Heat and a soft back change the plan. If he hunches or cries when he jumps at the porch step, the trail week ends and the clinic week starts. Young dogs get shorter drags on grass while they grow into the shorter height variety or the taller one that finishes at 15 inches. Sound adults may take a Saturday along a field edge, line on, water packed, home before the howling turns into a sore foot.</p>
<p>A bored scenthound escapes. A tired one sleeps through the compost truck. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/beagle/">AKC notes on the Beagle's nose</a> describe the hunter I am trying to tire, and the fence hardware is the other half of the same week.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The folder opens before the deposit. I want to know the variety named out loud: the one at 13 inches or below, or the one past 13 inches and through 15. I want the parents' weights, because adults in my notes settle from 20 pounds through 30, and a puppy who is already heavy at ten weeks is a conversation. DNA testing for Musladin-Lueke syndrome belongs in that same folder when the line has run it. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC forms to hold for Beagle parents</a> cover the orthopedic papers I can actually read. A seller who has none of this and only a story gets a polite goodbye.</p>
<p>Month 3 on my scale is a gangly hound with ears too big for the head. Month 5 the appetite arrives in full and the ribs disappear if I free-feed. Meals are measured from the first week. Buyers hear me quote 10 to 15 years, which is a long time to carry extra pounds and a long time to own a voice. Ear handling starts immediately. I lift the leather, touch the flap, and pay the puppy, so vet visits later are boring.</p>
<p>The first year is also fence education. A puppy who squeezes under a gate will do it faster when a trail is hot at eight months. I walk the perimeter with him and I close every gap before I need to. Recall practice uses a check cord plus food, in a dull yard, before I ever try it near rabbits. Baying is praised when I ask for a speak and interrupted when it becomes a dusk concert. Pack manners with other dogs are supervised. A Beagle puppy wants a group. The group should not be the one teaching him to bolt.</p>
<p>Halfway through the year the chest has dropped a little and the nose has hired the brain. Stairs and wild jumping off furniture stay limited because discs are on the breed list. Nails, teeth, and a mock vet stand happen weekly while he is still small enough to hold. The paperwork from day one stays in the folder. The adult problems are easier when the puppy year already named them.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-shedding-guide.html": `<p>White hair on a navy blanket is the Beagle jacket letting go. The coat is short. The hairs that appear on the couch come from the outer coat along the ribs, the neck, and the thighs, one stiff strand at a time, plus a finer pale hair from the belly when he leans in. There is a light undercoat in some dogs, and spring makes it more obvious. It is still a short-coated shed, a scatter, a lint-roller problem, a washer problem.</p>
<p>On a finished body of 20 pounds to 30 the surface is enough to salt a car seat. I have owned these hounds through spans running 10 years to 15, and the shed did not stop when the face went gray. It stayed steady, with a bump after a bath when hairs that were sitting loose finally dropped. A hound glove on round 3 and again on round 5 pulls that hair before it jumps to the cushion. That is the method that keeps the couch ahead of the season.</p>
<p>Ear leather grows a few short hairs along the edge. They are a small part of the mess. The bulk is the trunk. Tri-color dogs leave black, brown, and white in different rooms, and white is the one guests notice. People hope a dark sofa will hide it. The dark sofa hides the black hairs and advertises the white ones. I vacuum along the wall side of the mattress, because a Beagle sleeps curled there and that is where the jacket leaves evidence. Sunday packing tape across the cushion lifts a stripe of the white hairs the vacuum missed.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/beagle/">AKC description of the Beagle jacket</a> describes a close short jacket. Short and close is why the hair you find was a needle yesterday and is a needle in the laundry today. Brush the hound, and the floor earns a quieter week.</p>`,

  "guides/beagle-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Families hear 10 to 15 years from me. What trims it is usually a stack of ordinary failures left alone: a buried ribcage from an appetite nobody measured, seizures with no log and no plan, a thyroid that went quiet, a back that blew out on a jump, ears so chronically infected the dog lived in pain. I have watched hounds outlast the near end of that span. They were the ones whose meals were scooped and whose yelp got a same-day answer.</p>
<p>Stage 3 and stage 5 of adulthood are my window for a thyroid panel and a fresh look at the ears even if everyone thinks the dog is fine. Epilepsy can arrive in the middle years. Hypothyroidism can creep in while the coat just looks a little coarse. Intervertebral disc disease can end the walking years in an afternoon. Cherry eye and a sore ear will not set the lifespan by themselves, and they still mark whether anyone is looking at the dog.</p>
<p>Musladin-Lueke syndrome is a line problem with DNA testing available in some pedigrees. It goes in the purchase file so year two holds fewer mysteries. Pounds over the 20 to 30 band I see on adults make every joint and every disc work harder for the rest of those 10 to 15 years. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/beagle">VCA context for Beagle years</a> sits beside the years I already give families. A measuring cup and a locked gate do more for that span than a speech about love.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-beagles-get.html": `<p>A wicket and a scale split this hound in two, which beats any guess made beside a bathroom mirror. One variety is 13 inches and under. The other stands over 13 and up to 15 inches. Adults I weigh, once the baby fat is gone, usually land between 20 and 30 pounds. A 13-inch dog can still be sturdy. A 15-inch dog can still look moderate. I trust the wicket and the scale together. A photo of a puppy on a couch tells me neither number.</p>
<p>The youngster is ears and appetite. At month 3 the head seems too big and the legs seem borrowed. At month 5 the food intake jumps and the ribs vanish if the bowl stays full. I measure every meal from the week he comes home. Medium-sized hounds in my records look filled out between month 10 and month 16. Height is mostly there first. Chest and thigh come after. People declare him finished at the first adult-looking month and then act surprised when the scale moves again.</p>
<p>Ribs should take a light touch to find, with a waist from above. The appetite will lobby against that for 10 to 15 years if you let it. I write the weight on a card after a walk and before breakfast so the number means something. Two puppies from the same litter can aim at different varieties. I ask the breeder which height the parents actually measure, then I watch the puppy's own leg and stop arguing with a wish.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/beagle/">AKC Beagle variety measurements</a> are the card in the folder. Buyers who only saw the sire on a grooming table misread height. I set a level at the withers and keep the head in a natural pose. A treat held high fakes an extra bit of leg. The same hound weighed after a long drink looks heavier than he did at dawn, and both figures go on one line with the date.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-health-issues.html": `<p>A murmur at the elbow of a young Rottweiler, heard before anyone complained about tiring, was subaortic stenosis introducing itself. He quits a game he started with enthusiasm, or he fades on a familiar block, or he faints. I want a cardiac workup with a real listen and, when the murmur is present, imaging. Osteosarcoma is the other adult fear I plan around: a limp that refuses to match a pulled muscle, swelling along a long bone, worse on Thursday than it was on Tuesday. Radiographs of that limb are the pictures I ask for the same week.</p>
<p>Hips and elbows both fail through dysplasia. The owner sees a bunny hop behind, a slow push up from the floor, or a front foot that flips and wears the nails flat. Film 1 covers the pelvis. Film 2 covers the elbows. I want both in the file. Cranial cruciate ligament disease arrives as a sudden hind-leg limp, the dog sitting with the toe turned out, often after an ordinary turn in the yard. An orthopedic exam names it. I have iced that knee on my own kitchen floor while the surgeon's schedule caught up.</p>
<p>Bloat does not wait for morning. A tight abdomen, unproductive retching, and a dog who cannot settle are the picture, and the heavy working chest gives the stomach room to twist. Meals in my house stay away from hard running. I know the route to the emergency door before I need it. Juvenile laryngeal paralysis and polyneuropathy has DNA testing. A young dog with noisy breathing and a weak, uncoordinated stride is the dog I want that test run on, and I want the parents' results in hand before a puppy ever comes home.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck pages on subaortic stenosis and bloat</a> are what I send when a family has met either crisis and needs the vocabulary. Training and socialization keep the dog livable. They do not replace the heart check, the bone films, or the stomach plan. The body is large, the will is large, and the chart has to be large enough to match them.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-temperament.html": `<p>Guarding arrives whether anyone schedules it. Mine planted himself between me and every delivery van before he had a full adult mouth. Useful at a door. Expensive on a sidewalk if nobody has taught a greeting. Socialization starts in the early months, while he is still easy to move, and training starts the same week. A Rottweiler who meets the world only through a window becomes a dog who prosecutes the world later, with a body that can finish the argument.</p>
<p>Class 1 in my house is a down on a blanket while people pass the leash. Class 2 is a recall off the fence and a walk past strangers who are allowed to exist without a comment from him. I pay those behaviors with food and with access to the door, which he values. Harsh shouting makes a guardian louder. A clear marker and a job he can finish make him proud of the assignment I actually gave.</p>
<p>The build is heavy and the mind stays close. He wants the room I am in. Leave him from breakfast until supper and the invented work starts: a shredded bed, a patrol groove by the gate, a bark that is really a request for a person. I hand him a task every evening with a start and a finish. Adults keep the loyalty puppies show, plus more certainty about who belongs on the property and who is only passing.</p>
<p>Visitors get a serious once-over and then, if the introduction was boring and fair, he wants to be against my leg while I write charts. Affection looks like contact and like watching my face. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/rottweiler/">AKC Rottweiler guardian outline</a> matches the dog who sleeps with his back on my chair and still checks the hall when the floor creaks. Muscle without a handled job writes its own rules. Early manners are the whole plan.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Short hair over a double coat tricks people into owning zero tools. My hands start at the neck. Stroke 1 with a rubber curry lifts the guard hair and knocks yard dust off the shine. Stroke 2, the same curry pressed a little deeper, drags the undercoat out in soft tufts along the thigh, the chest, and behind the ear. A slick black jacket can hide a handful of loose wool until that wool is on the floor and on my pants.</p>
<p>I bathe him rarely. A mild wash and a long rinse clear the yard, and I towel the groin, the armpit, and the lip fold until those pockets are actually dry. A damp corner under a heavy body is odor by the next morning. Nails get clipped while he is still standing from the curry. Long nails change the foot on hardwood, and I can hear them from the hall before I see them.</p>
<p>The leather of the ear gets lifted every grooming sit. It holds warmth. I wipe the visible flap and smell the canal. Teeth get a brush while the mouth is already open for a treat. Feet get handled, pads included, since a seed rides home from a trail between them. My palm follows the curry over the ribs so a lump is felt before it is a phone call. Short hair makes skin easy to read when someone bothers to read it.</p>
<p>When the undercoat blows, the curry comes out most evenings for a couple of weeks. The rest of the year, two honest sessions keep the shine and the laundry in a truce. I part the coat along the sternum once a month and look for rash from a harness. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/rottweiler">VCA Rottweiler coat handling</a> lines up with the routine by my back door: a short coat that still has a second layer, and a person who has to go find it with their hands.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-exercise-guide.html": `<p>A working guardian's week is obedience, a real walk, and rest long enough for a heavy stomach to stay where it belongs. Dogs on the growth sheets who finish anywhere from 80 to 135 pounds still get the same shapes, scaled to the joints in front of me. Block 1 is heel, a sit at doors, and a settle while bicycles pass. I pause, timed from minute 8 to minute 10, before water and before block 2, so he avoids gulping air on a full stomach. Bloat is why that pause exists.</p>
<p>The next morning is a decompression walk, sniffing allowed, pace dull on purpose. Midweek repeats a shorter skill set in the yard: place, a recall, a down while the household moves around him. One day is a trot on grass if the elbows and hips are comfortable, and it ends before he looks cooked. One day is a handled greeting at the sidewalk, the guardian job done on my terms. One day is play with an off switch, a tug that ends when my hand closes. Another day is only a chew, then sleep. Hard running stays off the clock right after the bowl. Repeated launches off the porch stay off the clock entirely.</p>
<p>A tight belly or unproductive retching ends the week and starts the drive to the emergency door. A limp ends it too. Dysplasia in the hips and elbows, plus a cruciate that can go in one awkward turn, means I cancel a session when the gait looks wrong. Puppies and adolescents get a shorter outline while the frame is still building toward those adult pounds. Heat cuts the trot. Loyalty will keep a Rottweiler going past the point of sense, so I quit while he still looks keen.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/rottweiler/">AKC working background of the Rottweiler</a> is the page I show people who thought one loop of the block would spend this dog. One loop leaves him unfinished. His mind needs the assignment. The stomach and the joints need the quiet minutes already written into the week.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-puppy-guide.html": `<p>The folder opens before cash moves. I want hip and elbow imaging plans on the parents, a cardiac note because subaortic stenosis runs here, and DNA testing for juvenile laryngeal paralysis in the lines that offer it. The polyneuropathy piece is the same conversation. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC hip and elbow file for Rottweilers</a> is the packet I already know how to score. A kennel that fumbles those papers is a kennel I leave. Lesson 1 in the first month with me is a handled greeting. Lesson 2 is a down-stay on a blanket while the sidewalk passes the leash.</p>
<p>The body is already heavy while it is young. Males are aimed at 24 inches through 27. Females I measure are aimed at 22 inches through 25. That endpoint is why a four-month clown stays off solo jumps from the car and stays off long jogs on pavement. Growth plates are open. Stairs stay few. All-day hikes wait. Meals are measured so a palm can still find ribs while the chest deepens and the head broadens.</p>
<p>Company starts the first night. A puppy parked in a yard becomes a guardian with a grievance and no judgment. He comes to the kitchen, to one quiet sit in a parking lot, to a single new surface, then home. Teething gets a toy trade every time. Teeth on skin end the game. The adult will be strong enough to make a rude mouth dangerous. The puppy months are when the stop gets learned, while I can still enforce it with a finger and a cookie.</p>
<p>Halfway through year one strangers are already deciding he is either a threat or a teddy bear. Both readings skip the work. I keep the manners small and frequent, and I keep the medical folder where I can find birthday reminders for the heart. Nails, ears, and a practice vet stand happen while lifting him is still simple. Socialization, measured food, and papers already filed: that is the first year I will actually defend.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Two layers leave this dog, and they leave on different clocks. Coat 1 is the short guard hair, black with rust marks, stiff enough to stab fabric, leaving the ribcage, neck, and thighs as single stiff hairs all year. Coat 2 is the dense undercoat, softer, grayish when it packs a curry, blowing in spring and again in fall until the brush looks like a small animal. People who bought a short coat and expected an empty lint trap are living in a different house from mine.</p>
<p>Guard hairs show up in the driver's seat seam and in the cuff of a dark sweater, where the rust ones shout and the black ones hide until you stand up. The undercoat is the cloud that rolls along a baseboard after a shake. The softer belly coat transfers when he leans on a guest. The tail leaves a crescent on the car seat where he turns to watch traffic. I can tell which layer I am wearing by the texture on my palm.</p>
<p>A bath loosens both. The week after a wash I curry him in the evening or the bathroom drain files a complaint. Bare hands on a damp dog pull less than the tool. I do the curry on the patio and I still vacuum the threshold, because he walks back through his own shed. Pale floors advertise rust. Dark floors advertise the lighter undercoat. No upholstery I have tried wins that contest.</p>
<p>The curry lives by the door. During a blow I make slow passes down each hindquarter before he comes inside, and the laundry pile changes. Outside those weeks the shed is a steady spice of guard hair, easy to underestimate until a guest in light pants stands up copper-speckled. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/rottweiler">VCA notes on the Rottweiler undercoat</a> name a short outer coat plus a woolly second layer. Double is why a sleek dog can still fill a vacuum canister in March. The hair on the floor was growing yesterday from one of those two layers. Take it off him before it reaches the rug.</p>`,

  "guides/rottweiler-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The span I quote is 9 to 10 years. I start the senior conversation at year 8 anyway, because the problems that shorten a Rottweiler often speak before the birthday on the quote. A body that finishes between 80 pounds and 135 carries dysplasia of the hip, dysplasia of the elbow, and cruciate risk on every extra pound of fat or frantic weekend work. Osteosarcoma can take a dog who was trotting last month. Subaortic stenosis can take a dog whose puppy murmur was waved off. Bloat can take him after the evening meal and before midnight, tight through the belly, retching, unable to settle.</p>
<p>Screen 1 in the adult plan is the heart and the orthopedic films. Screen 2 is a hands-on look at both knees plus a kitchen-table review of the bloat signs, so nobody is inventing the plan at midnight. The DNA screen for that juvenile laryngeal condition, polyneuropathy included, goes in the whelping file. A youngster whose breathing is loud and whose legs wobble should arrive with that answer already attached, or with a plan to get it.</p>
<p>The dogs I watched arrive past year 9 and into year 10 had someone who noticed the first limp and the first cough and who refused to explain either one away as dust or as "just age." Meals stayed measured. Hard runs stayed away from the bowl. The guardian drive had a real job, so the dog was tired in the mind and sound in the joints more often than not. A July of frantic fetching on sore hips is an avoidable cut to a line that already reads short.</p>
<p>I tell families the digit 8 belongs on the calendar as a checkpoint, 9 is the year I see most often when a chart closes, and 10 is the far edge I am hoping for when the bones and the heart have been watched. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/rottweiler">VCA Rottweiler longevity factors</a> sit beside that quote. Affection does not add years by itself. The tests, the food scoop, and the decision to treat a limp as information do the adding, on the dogs where adding is still possible.</p>
<p>A weight somewhere between 80 pounds and 135 is only a clue. A lean dog at the top of that band can outlast a soft dog at the bottom. I keep a waist I can see from above and a rib I can find with slight pressure. That handling, more than any speech about devotion, is what I have seen move a Rottweiler toward the 10 and away from an early 8.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-rottweilers-get.html": `<p>Males I put a wicket on stand 24 to 27 inches. Females stand 22 to 25 inches. The pound span written across the growth sheets families bring me runs from 80 to 135, a heavy working build hung on those heights. Column 1 in the notebook is height at the withers, dog square, head in a natural carriage. Column 2 is weight that same morning, before the bowl, once he has paced the yard. The two figures describe one animal. A stacked show photo describes a wish.</p>
<p>He does not look like that adult for a long time. At month 8 the legs are most of the outline and the skull is only starting to suggest itself. At month 10 the appetite surges and the feet still look borrowed, which I trust more than a neighbor's announcement that he is "huge already." I photograph the scale. Recollection inflates every male into the biggest one in the waiting room.</p>
<p>Around month 12 the withers often stop climbing and people declare the project finished. The sternum is still shallow beside the adult he will become. Through the months after 12 and into month 18 the neck thickens and the chest drops. From there until month 24 the last muscle and the spring of rib fill in, males especially, sometimes pushing the top of that 80 to 135 pound band. Females often settle sooner, inside a lighter share of the same band. Meals stay scooped and jumping stays modest through the whole stretch so the frame finishes before a soft belly does.</p>
<p>A short coat makes overfeeding easy to miss. A sleek outline can still hide fat. I want a waist seen from above and ribs that take slight pressure, on the male near the 24-inch mark and the female near 22. A treat held too high lengthens the neck and lies about height, so the tape waits until he stands easy. After dinner the scale sits higher than the dawn reading. Both weights go on the line with the date, or I am lying to myself.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/rottweiler/">AKC Rottweiler height figures</a> stay taped inside the whelping notes. Shoppers who only shook hands with the sire misread the whelp standing in front of them. A four-month snapshot cannot show a 27-inch male or a 25-inch female. The fill that continues out toward month 24, after the early height at month 12 and the chest work around month 18, is the part I describe when someone meets a pretty adolescent and thinks the final pounds are already sitting on the scale. Those final pounds have yet to arrive. He is still becoming the measurement.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-health-issues.html": `<p>Noisy breathing is the soundtrack, and I stop calling it cute when the gums go dusky or the dog quits a short walk to lie down and stare. Brachycephalic airway disease is the name for that struggle: a nose that is pushed short, a long soft palate, and a throat that narrows when the demand for air rises. Heat makes it worse. Excitement makes it worse. I ask the practice to assess the airway while he is calm enough to be examined, and I want a plan for the day the noise changes character. Heatstroke is the emergency twin: panting that does not move air, a stagger, a collapse, fine in the kitchen and in trouble in a sunny car.</p>
<p>Facial wrinkles trap moisture. Skin-fold dermatitis smells sour, looks red, and feels slick. I clean, I dry, and if the skin breaks open the clinic sees it the same day. Cherry eye prolapses as a pink mass at the inner corner. Entropion rolls a lid inward so lashes scratch the eye. Squinting is the clue at home. An eye exam covers both problems, and I photograph the eye before the swelling changes the story.</p>
<p>Pelvic dysplasia shows up constantly. I have watched Bulldogs bunny-hop across a clinic room and still wag. Pelvic radiographs are the pictures I want on file before anyone debates powders and promises. Cesarean delivery is the usual way these litters start, so the dam's day was often surgical, and I ask about that history because it tells me how the litter was actually managed. Exam 3 and exam 5 with a dog who is new to me are when I repeat the airway questions and the hip questions even if the family feels they have already heard the lecture.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/bulldog">VCA Bulldog airway and fold guide</a> is the printout I hand over with a clean cloth for the wrinkles. The cloth is the home job, every face fold, dried after. The airway, the eyes, and the hips are the clinic job. A short smooth coat does not make this a low-maintenance body. The maintenance is just hidden in the nose, the folds, and the pelvis.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-temperament.html": `<p>Mine followed me from room to room at a walk and then dropped, content, against my shin. The temperament I can stand behind starts from the body: low exercise tolerance, a pull to be near a person, and a stubborn pause when breathing becomes the whole job. Push the outing and the mood sours into heavy panting and a sit-down strike. Keep the day cool and brief and the same dog is comic, affectionate, and thoroughly present.</p>
<p>Quiet hours land on turn 3. A short cool outing lands on turn 5, and only when the air is kind. The rest of the companionship happens indoors. He watches the kitchen. He snores through a phone call. He objects, using his weight, when a hand hits a sore wrinkle. That objection is data. I stop, I dry the fold, I return. Training that ignores the airway manufactures a fight and calls it stubbornness.</p>
<p>The undershot jaw and the wrinkled face make guests expect a clown and then act shocked by the opinions. I teach a down on a mat over a cool floor. Visitors get a sniff, then he usually wants the shade beside the chair. Children who climb on him get redirected. A dense body on dysplastic-prone hips is a poor jungle gym, however cheerful the tail is. Cheerful describes the mood. It does not describe the structure.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bulldog/">AKC Bulldog companion sketch</a> matches the dog who prefers the room to any athletic plan. I build the week around that preference. Forced into a busy sporting schedule, he becomes a miserable breather with a grudge. Given a person and a cool floor, he becomes the roommate people actually mean when they tell the story later.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-grooming-guide.html": `<p>The jacket is the easy minute. A short smooth coat takes a rubber curry and a damp cloth, and then every fold that can hold water becomes the real appointment. A male around 50 pounds and a female around 40 share the map: wrinkles over the nose, folds beside the eyes, the lip fold where food collects, and any damp pocket under the tail. The routine still runs at year 8 and at year 10, because older skin gets more fragile and stays just as needy.</p>
<p>Wipe 3 and wipe 5 of the week are the deep cleans. A soft cloth, a fold wipe made for dogs, and then a dry towel. Moisture left in the crease is how dermatitis starts its smell. I lift the nose roll. I raise the lip. I smell my fingers. Sour means I repeat it the next day, and I call the clinic if the skin is raw or bleeding. The curry on the back lifts loose hair and lets my palm find lumps the wrinkles are hiding from a casual glance.</p>
<p>Baths stay occasional and fussy. Water that enters a fold gets chased out with a towel corner. Room air will not finish that job. Nails stay short, because a heavy body on long nails twists the foot every step. Clipping happens in that same curry sit, which avoids a second wrestling match over the nails. The face is never sprayed straight on. Eyes get a damp cotton pad moved outward. Ears get a look even though guests assume the wrinkles are the only project.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/bulldog">VCA wrinkle cleaning for Bulldogs</a> matches the caddy by my sink: cloths, a dry towel, and the habit of finishing the fold before I finish the dog. Skip the drying and the smell is back by evening, on females around 40 pounds and males around 50 the same way. The smooth coat will look fine in a photo while the fold is already unhappy. My hands are there to disagree with the photo.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Heat writes the calendar, and I obey it. Airway trouble and heatstroke ride on a low tolerance for work, so the week is short cool movement plus a lot of rest. Loop 3 is a morning walk to the end of the block and back, before the pavement holds heat, with nothing tight on the throat. Loop 5 is the same distance at dusk if the air has actually dropped. Midday is a cool floor indoors. That is a full plan, and it is enough.</p>
<p>Other days stay smaller. A stroll to the mailbox. A sniff around the yard. A puzzle toy while I cook. I stop at the first heavy pant that looks inefficient, chest working, dog still starved for air. Play that spikes excitement can close the airway as surely as a hill in July. Greetings stay calm. Fetch that turns into a gasp leaves the program. Recovery is most of the week, and it is the productive part. Rested, he breathes better and offers better manners. Pushed, he becomes an overheated lump with a firm opinion.</p>
<p>Young dogs get even less distance while they grow toward about 50 pounds in a male or about 40 in a female. Summer can erase the dusk loop for an adult entirely. Water stays down. The car never sits in the sun with him in it. I test pavement with my palm. If I cannot leave my hand there, he cannot walk there. Shade is a destination with a job.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bulldog/">AKC Bulldog activity limits</a> are what I show people who arrived holding a jogging plan. This body was built for company at a slow pace, in air that lets the nose work. The week I will stand behind keeps him social, comfortable, and out of the emergency clinic in July. Anything louder is a story I do not want to write.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Cesarean birth is how most of these puppies meet daylight, often in a surgery suite. I ask how the dam recovered before anyone discusses price. Pelvic films on the parents matter because hip dysplasia shows up at a very high rate here. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC hip records for Bulldog litters</a> are the papers I can read. A seller with no films and a stack of adjectives gets a polite no. Week 3 at home is sleep, a cool crate, and tiny grass trips. Week 5 adds a practice exam stand plus a wipe of the nose roll, paid with food, so wrinkles stay boring for life.</p>
<p>The airway is already his problem. Eating can be noisy. I watch gum color on ordinary days so I know his normal pink. The house stays cool. Cars and sunlit pens are off the list entirely. Movement is a few minutes on grass, then rest. Adult targets I mention out loud are about 50 pounds for a male and about 40 for a female, and the puppy looks dense early. Density is a reason to slow down, never a reason to jog him around the block to "tire him out."</p>
<p>Folds start shallow and deepen. I clean and dry them while he is small enough that the argument is short. Food gets wiped off the lip after meals, or the fold becomes a rash I caused. Nails stay short. Teething gets legal chews. Parking his body on a towel starts now, because the adult is strong in a low, heavy way and miserable to reposition if he never practiced being moved.</p>
<p>Halfway through the year strangers are charmed and I am still counting breaths on mild afternoons. Panting at rest on a cool day earns a clinic visit the same afternoon. The hip paperwork stays clipped to the contract. The cool floor stays available. That is the puppy year I actually run, wrinkles, airway, and all.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Short hairs on a dark blanket come off the back, the flanks, and the thighs. The coat is smooth, so each hair is a single pale or brindle strand, a little scratchy, easy to miss until a guest in black sits in the Bulldog's spot. Nobody should expect a woolly seasonal blizzard. Shedding stays steady and light all year, with a modest rise once a bath lets perched hairs fall into the tub.</p>
<p>A rubber curry or a hound glove on photo 3 and again on photo 5 of the week shows me how much was about to land on the chair. Wrinkles contribute a mix of fine hairs and skin debris, which is why a cloth comes away gray even when the back looked clean in the window light. The face is mostly skin. The trunk is where the coat hair is actually made and released. I keep those two facts separate when someone asks why the face cloth and the lint roller tell different stories.</p>
<p>Fawn and white hairs shout on navy. Brindle hides until sunlight hits the fabric. I vacuum the arms of the chair he claims and the ledge under the window. A damp rubber mitt on the cushion collects strands the vacuum skipped. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bulldog/">AKC Bulldog smooth-coat line</a> talks about a sleek short jacket. Sleek is why the lint gets underestimated. The hair was growing from the outer jacket yesterday. It is a quiet shed. It is still hair, and it still belongs on the dog until the glove moves it.</p>`,

  "guides/bulldog-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>Families get 8 to 10 years from me, and I talk about heat in that same breath. Brachycephalic airway disease and heatstroke pull Bulldogs under that span when summer is treated like a sport and the snore is treated like a joke. Pelvic dysplasia is widespread in the breed, and it can make the middle of those years painful while the dog is still enthusiastic about dinner. Skin-fold dermatitis left wet and raw adds infection to a body that already works for each breath. Males I weigh near 50 pounds and females I weigh near 40 carry that dense mass on joints that were cramped from the start.</p>
<p>At year 3 and year 5 I want the airway reassessed and the hips discussed again, even when the family can recite the lecture from memory. Cherry eye and entropion will not set the clock alone. A dog who cannot see comfortably moves less and gets heavier, and both the hips and the airway resent the extra mass. Most of these dogs were delivered by cesarean. That birth fact does not forecast the adult span. The adult span tracks heat, the fat over the ribs, and whether anyone listened when the breathing changed pitch.</p>
<p>The dogs I saw push past year 8 toward year 10 lived in cool rooms, walked at dawn, had dry facial folds, and stayed lean enough that a palm could find a rib under the smooth coat. A July hike, a shut car, a heavy pad of fat over the loin: those are the cuts I have watched land. Note 3 in my own file is the last heat event. Note 5 is the last time someone said the limp was "just how Bulldogs walk." Both notes predict the chart better than the birthday cake does.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/bulldog">VCA Bulldog year-span context</a> sits next to the 8 to 10 I already say. Comfort is the longevity plan: cool air, dry wrinkles, a measured cup, and hips that were at least photographed. There is no alternate plan that involves ignoring the nose and hoping the dog is an exception. The exceptions I met were managed. They were not lucky in a closed car.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-bulldogs-get.html": `<p>Females I weigh as adults sit near 40 pounds. Males sit near 50. Those are the figures I give when someone asks how big this dog gets, and I attach the shape in the same breath: a wrinkled face, an undershot jaw, a nose pushed short, a dense low body. Pounds and skull are the conversation. A tape at the shoulder is a side note people use to feel organized. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/bulldog/">AKC Bulldog weight figures</a> stay in the puppy packet so a charming photo stops pretending to be a scale.</p>
<p>The youngster looks like a loaf earlier than other puppies. At month 3 the wrinkles are already a daily project and the chest is wide for the weeks he has been alive. When month 5 arrives, hunger can outrun the airway if the bowl is bottomless, so I measure every meal and I count breaths after he eats. My notes show the body settling from month 10 into month 16. Some look done when month 10 arrives and then add a surprising thickness by month 16. The undershot jaw is obvious long before the final pounds. People fall for the face and forget to book a weigh-in.</p>
<p>Ribs hide under a smooth coat and a broad chest. Hands do the reading a glance will fake. Slight pressure should find them, on the female near 40 and the male near 50. Extra fat stacked over those ribs makes breathing harder and hips angrier for a dog who already bargains for air. Midday heat and couch launches stay out of the growth months. A cool floor and a measuring cup do more for the adult outline than any speech about potential.</p>
<p>Buyers who met only the dam on a sofa misread mass every time. I weigh after a quiet indoor stroll, write the date, and decline to argue with a wish. Two pups can head for different ends of that 40 to 50 pound idea, one lighter female and one heavier male. The face will charm either way. The number on the scale is what the nose and the hips will carry for the rest of the 8 to 10 years I am willing to quote. Cute does not reduce the pounds. The scale is the only honest witness in the room.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-health-issues.html": `<p>Stiffness on the first steps out of the crate, or a hop of both hind feet when the trot begins, is how hip trouble introduces itself in the pointers I have hunted beside. I want hip radiographs read by someone who scores them often. Dysplasia changes a full day of quartering by middle age. I watched one fast dog shorten every cast to the left because one socket complained, and the owner saw the cheat in the grass long before any yelp. Topic 1 on my list is those hips together with the stomach, because both can end a working life.</p>
<p>Bloat is the emergency that can ruin a good season before the truck is unloaded. A deep-chested gundog who hunts hard, eats, then drinks in a gulp is the dog I worry over on the drive home. At home the belly drums tight, the retches bring nothing up, he cannot settle, and he stares at his flank. That picture means the emergency door. Waiting for morning is how stories end badly. Meals at my house stay clear of the hard run. Water after work comes in pauses. One frantic bowl stays off the list. The <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck bloat and gastric dilatation notes</a> are the pages I text a new pointer person before opening day, so the vocabulary exists when the night goes wrong.</p>
<p>Topic 2 is bleeding, swollen pups, and ears. Von Willebrand disease in some lines looks like a seep that continues after a torn nail, a heat cycle that seems far too heavy, or a dental extraction that will not clot on a normal schedule. I ask which test the line uses and what the parents returned, before any surgery date gets inked. A chemistry panel can look ordinary while clotting is the piece that fails. I have postponed a dental over a blank answer. I will postpone the next one too.</p>
<p>Lymphedema is reported in puppies of some lines. The picture I respect is swelling, often along the legs or over the brisket, in a youngster who is otherwise trying to grow. I want that pup seen, and I want a buyer told before any attachment forms if the line has produced it. Ears are the ordinary daily piece. Drop leather holds seeds, pond water, and wax. I lift the flap after field work, wipe what is visible, and smell. Head shaking the evening after a hunt means I book the clinic before the night is over.</p>
<p>The owner's list is doable: watch the first steps, memorize the bloat picture, ask about the bleeding test, look at every pup's legs in a litter with a history, and dry the ears before the truck door shuts. I write topic 1 and topic 2 on the discharge sheet so none of it depends on memory after a long day. A German Shorthaired Pointer keeps working while something hurts. Keenness is a poor diagnostic, and I stopped trusting it years ago.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-temperament.html": `<p>The first time mine locked up on a bird he forgot I was in the field. One foreleg lifted, tail a straight line, whole body aimed at a grass clump that looked empty. That freeze is the job. A pointing gundog was built to quarter for hours and to hold until a person arrived. From 45 pounds on a smaller female to 70 on a big male, the motor matches. The years I quote are 10 to 12, and I did keep one hunter who was still quartering at 14. The point was intact. The off switch was the skill I had to build on purpose.</p>
<p>Hunt 1 in my week is a long cast across an open field with a check-in paid in food. Hunt 2 is shorter and on cue, bumpers or birds, ended while he still wants another minute. A pointer who only stops when he collapses learns that stopping means falling down. I want stopping to be a behavior he can offer. Recall off a hot scent is drilled on a check cord before I ever trust a horizon. Independence made him useful on wild birds. The same independence will empty a poorly latched gate.</p>
<p>After the work he wants the truck, the kitchen, the rug with a view of the door. An idle day with no assignment becomes a shredded cushion or a fence line worn to dust. I give him a problem with an ending: find a hidden glove, hold a place while I answer mail, retrieve to my hand and release. Volume does less than a clean marker. He learns the pattern fast and then slacks off if I slack off. I keep the standard of the work as high as his stamina, or the stamina wins and the manners evaporate.</p>
<p>Puppies point butterflies and then break. Adults point with intent and still need manners wrapped around the statue. Both will outlast a casual weekend plan. Food works as pay. So does the chance to hunt again tomorrow, which he understands better than a lecture. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shorthaired-pointer/">AKC pointing-dog portrait of the German Shorthaired Pointer</a> matches the animal who sleeps like a stone after a real hunt and paces the house if the hunt was only a wish I mentioned out loud. Hours of work are the temperament. A smaller week has to be designed, or he designs a worse one.</p>
<p>I still sit with him in the evening after hunt 1 or hunt 2 and put a hand on the ribs until the breathing slows. The point and the pace are the public dog. The dog who can settle in the house is the one I can live with for those 10 to 12 years, and for the rare one who is still casting at 14. Without the settle, the gift becomes a grievance. With it, the same drive is the best company I have had in a truck.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-grooming-guide.html": `<p>Harsh short hair tells on a lazy week. Liver and liver-and-white both show dirt the minute they hit mud. Swipe 1 with a hound glove lifts dust and a few hard hairs off the ribs. Swipe 2 covers the thighs and the sternum, where burrs hide flat against the skin. Females near 45 pounds and males near 70 take the same path. The male simply owns more acreage. My palm follows the glove. A scab, a tick, or a thin patch is easier to feel on this jacket than to spot from the porch.</p>
<p>Ears are the grooming step that prevents the medical step. After grass or water I lift the leather, wipe the flap, and hunt for a seed he cannot scratch loose. A sour smell ends the home session and opens an appointment. Baths stay uncommon. Mud leaves a harsh coat once water reaches the skin. I dry the canals and the groin before any furniture privilege returns. Damp ear leather following a bath is a problem I have manufactured and then had to treat, and I am tired of manufacturing it.</p>
<p>Nails get clipped in the same sit as the glove. Field work can wear the toe nails while a dewclaw grows into a hook that grabs blankets. I brush teeth once he is up and waiting for a treat. White patches on a liver-and-white dog show saliva and grass stains. I wipe the muzzle. A solid liver dog hides the same stains until sunlight hits the lips. Either color, the hands do the inspection. The coat color will not file a report.</p>
<p>A towel and an ear wipe live in the truck door, and I use them before he loads. Waiting until the drive ends mashes debris deeper. The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/german-shorthaired-pointer">VCA harsh-coat care for German Shorthaired Pointers</a> matches that bucket: glove, towel, ear cloth, and a person who actually opens the bucket. Wash-and-wear is a fair description only on the weeks the hands show up. The other weeks, the coat looks fine and the ear tells the truth later.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-exercise-guide.html": `<p>Hours are the unit. The original job is quartering ahead of a hunter until a bird is pointed and held. Whether the dog weighs 45 pounds or 70, the week still asks for that work, with rest written in because hips wear and stomachs twist. Run 1 is a long cast in a safe field, early, air still cool, a check-in every few minutes paid with food. Then shade or a crate, and no bowl yet. Run 2 comes later and shorter: a whoa, a bumper to hand, an ending while he is still sharp enough to hear it. Meals wait until the breathing is ordinary again.</p>
<p>The middle of the week changes shape on purpose. The long cast does not get cloned every morning. Midweek might be a trot beside a bike only if the stop already exists and the footing is kind to the joints. Another outing is a swim when he actually likes water, ears dried before the truck. Another is a yard search for a glove, nose on, distance short. Another is a ramble where sniffing matters and speed does not. Another is a chew, then sleep, with no guilt attached to the rest. Hard running stacked on a full bowl is how the bloat stories I hate get started. I keep the bowl and the run in separate chapters of the day.</p>
<p>A hop, a shortened stride, or a dog who looks at one hip ends the running. Dysplasia does not care that the dog still votes to go. Youngsters get shorter versions of those outings while they build toward adult mass. Heat cuts the cast earlier than my ambition does. A pointer will finish the job and then invent another job. I quit before the invented one. Water comes in several small offers. A drowning gulp at the tailgate stays off the plan.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shorthaired-pointer/">AKC field-dog notes on German Shorthaired Pointers</a> describe the stamina I am trying to spend on purpose. An unspent pointer spends it on the fence, the door, or the couch cushions. The week is how I pick the target. Rest is included because a blown stomach or a worn hip ends the hunting years faster than a skipped Saturday ever could. I would like the Saturday. I would like the dog more.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-puppy-guide.html": `<p>Packet 1 is on the table before any money moves: hip radiographs on the parents, plus a plain answer about the test the line uses for von Willebrand disease. Packet 2 is the question I ask while looking at the pen: has this pedigree produced lymphedema in puppies, and may I watch them move? Swollen legs or a puffy brisket is a talk I want before I am attached to a face. <a href="https://ofa.org/chic-program/">CHIC hip file before a German Shorthaired Pointer deposit</a> is the orthopedic stack I can actually score. Stories about famous field dogs do not replace films or a bleeding-test result.</p>
<p>If the pup is male, the adult I am growing toward finishes 23 to 25 inches and carries 55 to 70 pounds. Females I have raised settled between 21 and 23 inches, carrying 45 to 60 pounds. People mash those into one span from 45 to 70 and then act surprised by a light female or a heavy male. At month 12 I put a tape on him because a lot of the height is present while the chest is still narrow. Month 14 gets its own tape in the puppy book too, since a photo at a year can lie about heart girth. Meals stay scooped. All-day hunts wait. The first year belongs to growth. Hunting season can wait.</p>
<p>Ear handling and an off switch start the week he arrives. I lift the leather with a treat, wipe grass debris, and end play while he still wants it. Pointing shows up as a comic freeze on butterflies. I let the instinct live and I put a release on it, so a point stays a point and the puppy can still be asked to walk. Recall work happens on a check cord in a dull field. The horizon is too interesting to be the first classroom, and a pointer puppy will prove that by vanishing toward it.</p>
<p>A crate nap and real company are both on the schedule. Kennel-only pups become frantic adults. Couch-only pups become frantic adults with no plan for stamina. He rides along, searches for a glove, settles on a mat, and sleeps hard. Nails and teeth happen weekly while holding him is easy. By the time the chest drops, the folder already holds the parents' hips and the clotting answer, and the calendar already holds month 12 and month 14 as dates I refuse to skip. That paperwork is the deposit. The cute part is extra.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-shedding-guide.html": `<p>Harsh hairs leave one strand at a time, from the ribs, the neck, the thighs, and the tail of a German Shorthaired Pointer. Sweep 1 with a curry pulls the loose guard hair that was ready to drop. Sweep 2 finds a thinner supply of softer hair close to the skin, enough to notice, and nothing like the wool cloud a heavy double coat throws. Liver strands vanish on dark fabric and shout on a white truck seat. White strands do the reverse. A patched dog donates both, and the laundry sorts the argument without my help.</p>
<p>A long hunt loosens the jacket. That evening I glove him outdoors and watch the hair drift off the patio, hair that grew on the trunk and then got stabbed free by brush. A bath repeats the show a day later. People expect a short coat to mean a clean house. The bathroom floor after a rinse corrects the expectation. Belly hair is finer and paler, and it moves onto dark shirts when he leans in to be included, which is most evenings of his life.</p>
<p>Truck seams and the edge of the bed collect the evidence, because he sleeps stretched out, and the places his body touches are where the coat reports. A damp rubber glove on the cushion lifts what the vacuum leaves behind. The shed is year-round and modest, with a spring bump. It will not bury a room the way a thick undercoat can. It will salt every dark surface if the glove stays in a drawer. I keep the curry where my hand finds it when we unload, and I use it then, while the hair is still a field problem and not yet a couch problem.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/german-shorthaired-pointer">VCA shedding notes for the pointer coat</a> call the coat short and harsh. Harsh is why yesterday's needle is today's lint. Glove the dog and the floor gets a temporary truce. Skip it and you wear the liver ticked hairs to work and wonder where the white couch went. The hair was manufactured on the outer jacket. That is the whole geography of this shed.</p>`,

  "guides/german-shorthaired-pointer-lifespan-guide.html": `<p>The line I give is 10 to 12 years. Older kennel cards in a drawer still print 12 to 14, and I explain what cuts into either printing. Bloat can finish a dog after supper and well before dawn: deep-chested, fine at the truck, tight through the abdomen, retching with nothing to show. Hip disease can steal the working seasons before it steals the life, a pointer who still aims at the field and can no longer cover it. Von Willebrand disease can turn a routine dental or a torn quick into a bleed that will not stop. I have watched all three. None of them send a polite note a month ahead.</p>
<p>Factor 1 on my longevity list is the stomach. Meals stay away from hard runs, and someone in the house can recite the emergency picture without looking it up. Factor 2 is the hips and the clotting history: films while he is young enough for the pictures to be a plan, and a test result for the bleeding disorder before a surgery date is chosen. Lymphedema sits in the puppy chapter for some lines. A swollen pup can be the first hint that this pedigree wants extra eyes, so the note belongs in the early record even if the adult span is a separate question.</p>
<p>Wet infected ears will not set the span by themselves. A dog in ear pain hunts less, rests badly, and lives smaller, which is its own kind of shortening. The dogs I saw approach the far side of 10 to 12, and the few who touched the 14 on those old cards, had work that was steady and rest that was real. They were spared the pattern of six empty days and one enormous Saturday. They had a waist. Someone ended the cast while the dog still looked keen. That Saturday-only pattern is an avoidable cut, and I have seen it land on otherwise promising hips.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet/german-shorthaired-pointer">VCA year range for German Shorthaired Pointers</a> sits beside the 10 to 12 I say out loud, with 14 reserved for the old cards and the rare dog who got the management. The span is a range. The stomach, the hip films, and the bleeding history decide where inside it a particular pointer lands. I can influence those three. I cannot negotiate with a twisted stomach at midnight, so the influence has to happen at noon. Noon choices are the ones that still belong to the people who feed him.</p>`,

  "guides/how-big-do-german-shorthaired-pointers-get.html": `<p>A finished male stands 23 to 25 inches at the shoulder and weighs 55 to 70 pounds. A finished female stands 21 to 23 inches and weighs 45 to 60 pounds. Log 1 is the wicket, dog easy, chin level, no treat parked overhead to fake a neck. Log 2 is the scale the same morning, before breakfast. Light females at 45 pounds and heavy males at 70 pounds are why a single "pointer size" is a sloppy phrase I refuse to use with buyers. The <a href="https://www.akc.org/dog-breeds/german-shorthaired-pointer/">AKC male and female German Shorthaired Pointer measurements</a> replace the adjective. I read the numbers. I skip parking-lot stories about a sire who looked moderate.</p>
<p>The adolescent lies with a straight face. At month 12 the withers may already sit near the adult figure while the chest is a narrow draft of the dog he will become. Month 14 gets a second tape, and I almost always find more depth than new height. Between month 18 and month 24 the rib spring and the thigh fill, males especially, sometimes walking the scale toward the top of 55 to 70. Females often settle earlier inside 45 to 60. People who buy bowls, crates, and a truck pad for the month-12 body get surprised, pleasantly or cramped, by month 24.</p>
<p>Liver or liver and white does not move the tape. Color photographs well and fools the eye about mass. Hands find ribs. From above I still expect a waist, plus a tuck that says the dog can work, on the male near 25 inches and the female near 21. Extra fat over the loin gets excused because Saturday's run seems to erase it. Thursday's body is the one the hips and the stomach actually live with. I weigh on Thursday.</p>
<p>I watched a gangly pup at month 12 become a substantial gundog at month 24 with little added height and a lot of heart girth and ham. That fill is what I describe when someone holds a pretty adolescent and announces the final pounds. Tailgate jumps and all-day runs wait while the skeleton is the project. The stamina shows up later, and it is safer if the frame was allowed to finish. Log 1 and log 2, repeated, beat every guess I have ever heard in a parking lot. I write the Thursday weight in ink so a heroic Saturday cannot revise the record.</p>`,
};
